Environment (once)
Nested rootless Podman in a non-privileged pod (no SCC privileged: true)
Caps: SETUID, SETGID, SYS_CHROOT + allowPrivilegeEscalation: true
Custom seccomp (clone/unshare/mount/chroot/keyctl/setgroups/…)
/dev/fuse + /dev/net/tun via CRI-O Devices annotation
Working uid_map, e.g.
0 <openshift-uid> 1
1 100000 65536
Workaround that does run: netns=host, bind /proc, /dev/pts, /sys/fs/cgroup, nested podman run --privileged --user 0
Issue 1 — pasta / slirp / tun (primary)
Wanted: isolated nested net (pasta or slirp) and real -p publish.
Got: both backends fail on tun.

Evidence:

stat /dev/net/tun          # crw, 10:200, 0666
# python open in POD and podman unshare:
open_fail 19 ENODEV No such device: '/dev/net/tun'
pasta --debug -f /bin/true
# Failed to open() /dev/net/tun  OR  TUNSETIFF ...
# Failed to set up tap device in namespace
# slirp4netns: same open() failure
ls works; open(O_RDWR) does not — in the pod, not only in pasta’s ns. Kind + real hostPath tun: open() OK. This looks like a dead cgroup/injected node, not a missing path.

Tried: NET_ADMIN, pasta vs slirp — no change.

Questions:

Supported way to pass a working tun into a non-privileged OCP pod (open() succeeds)?
Is nested rootless pasta supported without privileged SCC?
If no, what is the supported networking model?
Issue 2 — --user 1000 / TTY (/dev/pts)
Wanted: nested container as image user (e.g. --user 1000), with -it.
Got: crun: chown /dev/pts/1: operation not permitted
Works: --user 0 (userns root = OpenShift UID).

We bind /dev/pts because nested mount(devpts) is denied.

Not seccomp:

podman unshare: chown /tmp to 0:0  → OK
podman unshare: chown /tmp to 1000:1000 → OK
Only chown on the bind-mounted pty fails.

--user $(id -u) (e.g. 1000900000) also fails: in this userns we are uid 0, not the OpenShift numeric id.

Questions:

Supported way to run nested --user 1000 with a TTY when /dev/pts is the pod devpts?
Is --user 0 or --userns=keep-id the expected model?
How should non-root image USER work without chown on host-owned pts?
